(1) DILATION INVARIANCE OF THE S_k (hence of BONF5):
    120 (subset, dilation) pairs, sizes 2-4: failures = 0
    => every S_k is dilation-invariant, so BONF5(k*V) = BONF5(V).
       A BONF5-failing family therefore dilates to ARBITRARILY LARGE
       speeds while still failing: the residual is UNBOUNDED as a set.
       The correct question is whether it is bounded UP TO DILATION,
       i.e. whether only finitely many PRIMITIVE families fail.

(2) SO: DO THE S362 FAILURES SURVIVE PRIMITIVITY?
    (if the small-speed failures were merely dilates, primitivity kills
     them; if they are genuinely primitive, the residual is real)
    [32, 60, 95, 144]: gcd = 1  PRIMITIVE
    [49, 55, 59, 72]: gcd = 1  PRIMITIVE
    [63, 88, 107, 186]: gcd = 1  PRIMITIVE
    [71, 72, 136, 158]: gcd = 1  PRIMITIVE
    [84, 91, 167, 200]: gcd = 1  PRIMITIVE
    [89, 118, 219, 322]: gcd = 1  PRIMITIVE

    => the S362 failures are primitive, so they are genuine residual
       members, not artefacts of a common factor.
